  "prose|var|Usage: hive-progress [--title <title>] [--card <card>] [--revision <commit>] -- <command> [args...] hive-progress --check <name> [--isolated] [--title <title>] [--card <card>] [--revision <commit>] Runs the command, passing its output through and returning its exit code, and shows it in Hive's Progress panel with the time it usually takes, and the card and revision it ran for. --card <card> the card it runs for (\"#1061\", quoted); without it, the card you are working on (yours in Doing) --revision <commit> the commit it runs on: it must be what is checked out here, or nothing runs; without it, what is checked out when it starts --check <name> runs the project's command for that check (the project's options name it), not one of yours; focused:<test> (a card's focused test) runs your command after -- as that test's check --isolated with --check: runs it in a check copy of its own at the card's revision, which nothing else writes, so another agent's edits here can't spoil it (waits for a copy when none is free) A line the command prints like ##hive-progress step=4 total=12 name=carddialog says step 4 of 12 (carddialog) is starting, and isn't shown. The first total given is the run's: give it within the first 2 seconds for a bar with steps from the start, or later for one from then on. ##hive-progress wait=a test slot (held by B4) says the command waits (for a queue, a lock) and for what: no time left is shown meanwhile, and the wait doesn't count against the usual time; \"##hive-progress wait=\" says it waits no more. A command interrupted by Ctrl+C shows as stopped, and one whose hive-progress is killed as ended without a report. Outside Hive it just runs the command. HIVE_PROGRESS=0 turns reporting off.": 1,
